        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"zuccoli-lamore-di-loredana-c7c2bf4492a62522\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Berto's repeated “Ma io ti amo ... Io ti amo, Loredana” is principally a direct avowal of his loving state, offered as the sole explanation for his conduct. It does not itself undertake a commitment, and the passion and repetition do not provide independent evidence of reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The avowal occurs within Berto's effort to make Loredana stay, giving it an indirect persuasive role, but that role does not make the core words a relational undertaking. Its emotional intensity likewise falls short of independent E force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Berto directly addresses Loredana with a paired, repeated avowal; the highlighted occurrence is the first “io ti amo” in that speech.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Loredana has just declared that she loves Filippo, hates Berto, and would never love him. Berto answers her destructive logic with love as the explanation for his seemingly irrational persistence. The narration explicitly presents him as sincere, while subdued and defeated, so the utterance is strongly truth-conditional in mode. Its surrounding function is pleading and persuasive, but the core avowal promises nothing. Berto's later coercive conduct complicates his characterization without changing the semantic-pragmatic force of this utterance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“poi ebbe la parola unica che spiegava qualunque follia: --Ma io ti amo ... Io ti amo, Loredana; e non so altro”\",\"supports\":\"The narration frames the love declaration as an explanatory avowal—Berto's sole answer for his conduct—strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“La giovane lo guardò, sentendo ch'egli non mentiva”\",\"supports\":\"Loredana perceives that Berto is not lying, corroborating the contextual sincerity of the state avowal, though T classification does not depend on actual truth.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately before the declaration, Loredana says that she loves Filippo, hates Berto, and will never love Berto.\",\"supports\":\"The declaration directly contrasts Berto's love with Loredana's rejection and functions as his answer to her question about why he wants her.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The words are repeated and delivered amid passion, but the text does not describe them as involuntarily blurted or produced before reflection.\",\"supports\":\"Supports E=0: intensity and repetition are present, but no independent reflexive or discharge-like core force is established.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense, explicit first-person-to-second-person love expression.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted core is a canonical present-state love avowal rather than a hypothetical or reported construction.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the utterance's central force as a sincere-seeming avowal. Its pleading context and emotional delivery can be described contextually without adding P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
